## Context

Author imagine (2026-06-06, during the 0446 figure review): "a transposed
version, portrait mode. Asset names easier to read, column names displayed
hierarchically on 3 lines: one model tag, then the version tag, then the
run tag."

Transposing puts the 170 plants on rows — names read horizontally (no 90°
rotation), which is the only orientation where they have a chance of being
genuinely readable. The 70 runs become columns with a 3-level hierarchical
header: family tag (claude / deepseek / gpt / mistral / qwen), then version
tag (opus-4.6, v4-pro, 5.5, ...), then run tag (r1..r5) — each level spans
its children, like a pivot-table header.

## Geometry to validate at Plan

170 rows on a portrait A4 text block (~9.7 in usable height) gives
~1.45 mm/row → plant labels at ~4 pt print. Readable is ~5-6 pt, so likely
needs one of: two-page split (e.g. by status group), full-page portrait
float with reduced margins, or a row subset. Measure before promising
"easier to read" — the 0446 arbitration already accepted texture-level
labels once; this variant only earns its keep if names become actually
legible.

## Pistes

- Same derivation (`exp1_recognition`), same band logic transposed: status
  bands become horizontal stripes with labels in the left margin (reading
  direction is natural there).
- Hierarchical header: family/version/run as three stacked text rows with
  spanning rules (matplotlib: text + hlines on a header band; no table
  machinery needed).
- Keep the FP panel as a bottom (or top) block of 40 rows, red.
- CLI: `--transpose` on plot_exp1_matrix, or a sibling script if the layout
  diverges too much to share cleanly.

## Exit criteria (to sharpen at Plan)

- [ ] Portrait variant builds via the DAG alongside the landscape one
- [ ] Plant names legible at print scale (measured, not asserted)
- [ ] 3-line hierarchical run header (family / version / run)
- [ ] Author picks which orientation ships in the manuscript annex

